# Tutorial 05 — Transitions and Effects
# [transition fade], [transition crossfade], [screen flash], [screen shake] — visual punctuation.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_05_transitions
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Transitions control what the player sees {i}between{/i} scenes. Fades, dissolves, flashes, shakes.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Each one is a single command with a duration in milliseconds. Let's cover all four.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> [transition fade] — fade through black -> section_fade
> [transition crossfade] — dissolve between images -> section_crossfade
> [screen flash] — burst of light -> section_flash
> [screen shake] — camera shake -> section_shake
> Duration guide — choosing the right speed -> section_timing
> Quick reference: visual punctuation -> quick_reference
> Practice: live effect demos -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use visual effects like punctuation, not wallpaper:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 500]{/color} — hard scene break.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition crossfade 700 field_evening]{/color} — continuous change.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen flash white 180]{/color} — impact or revelation.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen shake 8 250]{/color} — shock or instability.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: one effect per beat, short durations for impact, longer durations for mood.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_fade
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Fade ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_fade
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[transition fade N]{/b} fades the screen to black over N milliseconds, then reveals the new content.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 500]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg cafe_night]{/color}
narrator: Screen goes dark over 500ms. New background appears when darkness completes.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Fades signal a {b}hard scene break{/b} — a new location, a time skip, an act change.
Lavender: Add a [wait] between the fade and the new background to hold in darkness:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 800]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 500]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bg new_scene]{/color}
narrator: That extra 500ms of black creates anticipation. The player wonders what's coming.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Fade is the safest default transition. When in doubt, use fade.
> Next: crossfade -> section_crossfade
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Crossfade ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_crossfade
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[transition crossfade N id]{/b} dissolves the current background directly into a new one. No black screen.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition crossfade 700 field_evening]{/color}
narrator: The old image melts into field_evening over 700ms.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Crossfades signal {b}continuous change{/b} — same place, different time. Day to night, clear to rain, calm to storm.
narrator: {b}Short crossfade (300–500ms){/b} — brisk, modern. A quick lighting change.
narrator: {b}Long crossfade (800–1500ms){/b} — dreamy, slow. Time is passing visibly.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use crossfade when the location stays the same but the atmosphere shifts. Use fade when the location changes entirely.
> Next: screen flash -> section_flash
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Screen Flash ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_flash
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[screen flash intensity duration R G B]{/b} — a momentary burst of color across the screen.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen flash 0.25 120 1 1 1]{/color}
narrator: {b}intensity{/b} 0.25 — how bright (0.0–1.0). {b}duration{/b} 120ms. {b}RGB{/b} 1 1 1 = white.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Common uses:
narrator: {b}White flash{/b} (1 1 1) — lightning, explosions, magical bursts, camera flash.
narrator: {b}Red flash{/b} (1 0 0) — damage, injury, alarm.
narrator: {b}Warm flash{/b} (1 0.9 0.5) — sunlight breaking through, warm memory.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep flashes under 200ms. Longer flashes feel like a bug. The player should barely register it consciously.
> Next: screen shake -> section_shake
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Screen Shake ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_shake
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[screen shake intensity duration]{/b} — rattles the entire view.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen shake 0.3 200]{/color}
narrator: {b}intensity{/b} 0.3 — how far it moves (0.0–1.0). {b}duration{/b} 200ms.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Uses:
narrator: {b}Light shake (0.1–0.2, 100–150ms){/b} — a flinch, a door slamming, a stumble.
narrator: {b}Medium shake (0.3–0.5, 150–250ms){/b} — an explosion, a hit, something breaking.
narrator: {b}Heavy shake (0.6+, 200–400ms){/b} — earthquake, catastrophe. Use {i}very{/i} sparingly.
Lavender: Combine with a flash for maximum impact:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen shake 0.4 180]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen flash 0.3 100 1 1 1]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: One flash-shake combo per scene at most. More than that and the player goes numb.
> Next: duration guide -> section_timing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Duration Guide ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_timing
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Every transition effect takes a duration in milliseconds. Picking the right number matters more than the effect itself.
narrator: {b}100–250ms{/b} — instant. Flashes, quick shakes. The player feels it before thinking about it.
narrator: {b}300–500ms{/b} — snappy. Fast scene changes, location hops, upbeat moments.
narrator: {b}500–800ms{/b} — standard. Most transitions live here. Comfortable and cinematic.
narrator: {b}800–1200ms{/b} — slow. Dramatic reveals, emotional weight, act breaks.
narrator: {b}1200ms+{/b} — very slow. Opening/closing sequences, major story beats only.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: When in doubt, 500ms is a safe default for fades and crossfades. Adjust from there.
> Practice: live demos -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I'll run three effects live so you can see and feel each one. Watch the screen.

@label practice_demo
Lavender: {b}Demo 1: Fade{/b} — a scene change.
[wait 300]
[transition fade 600]
[wait 400]
{{BG_CROSSFADE}}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That was a 600ms fade with 400ms held in darkness.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Demo 2: Flash + Shake{/b} — an impact.
[wait 300]
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Something's coming—
[screen shake 0.35 200]
[wait 50]
[screen flash 0.25 100 1 1 1]
[wait 200]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Shake 0.35 for 200ms, then flash 0.25 for 100ms. Two lines of script.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Demo 3: Slow fade{/b} — a dramatic pause.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: And then... everything went dark.
[transition fade 1200]
[wait 800]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: 1200ms fade, 800ms in the dark. The slowness made you wait. That's the point.

[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's the script for all three demos:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 600] + [wait 400]{/color} — standard scene change.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[screen shake 0.35 200] + [screen flash 0.25 100 1 1 1]{/color} — action impact.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[transition fade 1200] + [wait 800]{/color} — dramatic hold.
Lavender: Edit the numbers in this file and replay to feel how timing changes the mood.
> Replay demos -> practice_demo
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
